Dev log — July 17, 2026, PitWall Day 1

Finally started PitWall for real.

Set up the VS project, SQLite via sqlite3.h, decided on a
domain/repository/service split (repo only knows domain stuff,
storage handles raw SQL, service ties it together). Spent a while
making sure I actually understood the direction of that chain and
wasn't just repeating it.

Built the Node hierarchy — Driver/Team/Circuit as subclasses,
polymorphic via get_type_string(). Went back and forth on what
data actually belongs on a node. First instinct was to throw in
nationality, logos, etc., but caught myself — that's UI/SQL stuff,
not graph stuff. Nodes only hold the math the Markov engine
actually needs (tire_management_modifier, base_pace_delta, etc.).
Killed StrategistNode for now since I don't know what it'd even
hold yet.

Edge struct has winRate, affinityScore, performanceDelta, and
tirePreference. Almost made it an unordered_map keyed on
pair<int, int> — turns out pair has no default hash, would've
broken. Used a regular map instead.

Graph itself keeps both successor and predecessor maps. Felt
redundant at first, but otherwise removing a node means scanning
the entire graph to find incoming edges. Reused this from the
graphs lab I did earlier this year, just adapted it to hold real
Node objects instead of bare ints.

Got it compiling after two dumb mistakes:

- Include path was wrong because Solution Explorer shows folders
  that don't actually exist on disk (VS lets you fake folder
  structure visually). Learned that the hard way.

- Updated DriverNode's constructor to take new fields, then forgot
  to update the make_shared call using it. Compiler complained way
  down in some xutility internals file, which was confusing until
  I realized it was just a constructor mismatch.

Two tests passing: node creation/polymorphism, and edge
add/get/is_edge.

Still missing remove_node/remove_edge. I know exactly how they
should work (predecessor scan, clean both maps, drop from costs
too), just haven't coded them yet. That's next.


Dev Log — July 23, 2026

PitWall — Phases 1-4 completed and verified

What got done:

Fixed DFS (was declared but never implemented) — iterative stack-based traversal
Added remove_node() — cleans registry, adjacency maps, and incident edges
Implemented Dijkstra for shortest-path queries
Built SQLite persistence via Repository pattern (vcpkg-managed, not vendored) — full save/load round-trip, including a tricky ID-gap bug when nodes get removed before saving
Built the data import pipeline: Python script pulls the real 2024 F1 season from the Jolpica API, C++ DataImporter reads it into the graph
10/10 tests passing. Real data loaded: 59 nodes (25 drivers, 10 teams, 24 circuits), 479 edges with computed win rates
Pushed everything to GitHub

Bugs squashed:

MSVC narrowing/aggregate-init errors (C2397/C2512) from sloppy Edge brace-init
Stale debug .db file causing abort() on repeat test runs
Wrong Jolpica API domain (was api.jolpica.com, should've been api.jolpi.ca)
API pagination cutting results off at 100 instead of full season (needed ?limit=1000)

Where it stands: Graph engine + SQLite + real F1 data all working end to end. 
First time PitWall processed actual race data instead of test fixtures.

Next up: Phase 5 — Markov transition model. Scoping down from the full (driver, circuit, lap, tire, position) state space to a (driver, circuit, grid_position) → 
finish_position model, since one season isn't enough data to populate the full space meaningfully.
